<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valparaíso</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valparaíso is a port city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, built on steep hills above a bay on the Pacific. Founded by the Spanish in 1536, it became the main commercial port of the South American Pacific in the nineteenth century, before the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a>. It was bombarded by a Spanish squadron in 1866, its historic quarter is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and it has been the seat of Chile's Congress since 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valparaiso.jpg" alt="Valparaíso" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julia Sumangil · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_Quarter_of_the_Seaport_City_of_Valpara%C3%ADso_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Valparaíso lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, about 120 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, on dozens of steep hillsides overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. Its proximity to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>–Chile Trench, which stores large amounts of energy and sometimes ruptures in violent earthquakes, makes it vulnerable to them. The August 1906 earthquake devastated the city and killed nearly 3,000 people, and the 1730 Valparaíso earthquake and the 1985 Algarrobo earthquake were also significant. Residents are called porteños, Spanish for 'port dwellers'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first identifiable people of the bay were the Picunche, known for their agriculture. Spanish explorers arrived in 1536 aboard the Santiaguillo, a supply ship sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> for his expedition. Its commander, Juan de Saavedra, named the town after his native village of Valparaíso de Arriba in Cuenca Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; the town's original name was Alimapu or Quintil. Through the colonial period it remained a small village of a few houses and a church, attacked at times by English privateers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> with the Golden Hind in 1578 and Richard Hawkins with the Dainty in 1594. Drake's sack of the town gave rise to the legend of the Cueva del Pirata.</p><p>The Chilean Navy has had its headquarters in the city since 1817. In the second half of the 19th century Valparaíso was a major stopover for ships travelling between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans by the Straits of Magellan, and in its golden age it drew many European immigrants and was known to sailors as Little San Francisco. It acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>'s oldest stock exchange, the continent's first volunteer fire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>'s first public library and El Mercurio de Valparaíso, the oldest newspaper in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> in continuous publication. The opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> in 1914 reduced ship traffic and dealt a serious blow to the port economy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Valparaíso is the seat of the National Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, which has met there since 1990, and the capital of the Valparaíso Region. Its historic quarter, a labyrinth of cobbled alleys and colourful buildings on the hills, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2003. Sixteen funiculars, called ascensores, remain, fifteen of them public and national monuments. The commune had 284,938 inhabitants at the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, within a Greater Valparaíso metropolitan area of 951,311.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valparaíso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Valparaíso</b> became the main commercial port of the South American Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The tsunami inundated the lower parts of <b>Valparaíso</b> and affected more than 1,000 km of Chile's coastline.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Off <b>Valparaíso</b>, Chile, the frigate HMS Phoebe and the sloop HMS Cherub fought the frigate USS Essex and the sloop USS Essex Junior.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-28">28 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
